phy: rockchip: inno-usb2: raise the RV1106 45ohm HS ODT trim to 5'b11111

The vendor value 5'b10111 leaves the high-speed link with too little margin on
at least one host. On the Ubuntu 24.04 build host (Linux 6.8.0-124, xHCI) the
board cannot be enumerated at all with the stock trim:

    usb usb5-port3: Cannot enable. Maybe the USB cable is bad?
    usb usb5-port3: attempt power cycle
    usb usb5-port3: unable to enumerate USB device

That message comes from hub_port_reset() in drivers/usb/core/hub.c after every
reset retry fails to see USB_PORT_STAT_ENABLE, so the link never completes reset
and no USB transaction is attempted: no address is assigned, no descriptor is
read. The host does register a connect, so the board is powered and its PHY is
driving a data-line pullup; it is the high-speed reset that fails. This field
trims the 45ohm HS termination that both ends enable after the chirp handshake,
and a termination that is off far enough makes the host's squelch and disconnect
detection trip during reset, which matches the observed stage of failure.

Evidence for this specific value, recorded because an earlier attempt at the
same change shipped without it:

  - The two images compared differ by this register alone. Everything else
    between them is documentation, the Buildroot overlay (which the Debian
    rootfs builder never reads), build-side hash bookkeeping, and a patch header
    that git apply ignores.
  - With 0x1f the board enumerates and stays at 480 Mbps on that host. With 0x17
    it fails as above on every retry cycle, with no successes.
  - BootROM Maskrom enumerates on the same host, which uses neither value, so
    the cable, the port and the host controller can train a link with this
    board. Maskrom is also why this patch cannot be judged by whether flash mode
    works: the Linux PHY driver does not run there.

Still unmeasured: there is no eye diagram or protocol-analyser capture, so the
margin this buys is not quantified, and 0x1f saturates the field rather than
being a vendor-recommended point. The failure signature is also worse than the
one seen in 2026-08 on the same host, which then enumerated before failing with
-71; the physical margin has degraded since. Treat 0x1f as the empirically
working end of a range that is known to be narrow, not as headroom.

Full investigation: docs/07-operations/rv1106-usb-high-speed-enumeration-incident.md
on the build host (that path is covered by a local .git/info/exclude rule and is
not in this repository).

diff --git a/sysdrv/source/kernel/drivers/phy/rockchip/phy-rockchip-inno-usb2.c b/sysdrv/source/kernel/drivers/phy/rockchip/phy-rockchip-inno-usb2.c
--- a/sysdrv/source/kernel/drivers/phy/rockchip/phy-rockchip-inno-usb2.c
+++ b/sysdrv/source/kernel/drivers/phy/rockchip/phy-rockchip-inno-usb2.c
@@ -2825,8 +2825,8 @@ static int rv1106_usb2phy_tuning(struct rockchip_usb2phy *rphy)
 	/* Turn off differential receiver by default to save power */
 	phy_clear_bits(rphy->phy_base + 0x100, BIT(6));
 
-	/* Set 45ohm HS ODT value to 5'b10111 to increase driver strength */
-	phy_update_bits(rphy->phy_base + 0x11c, GENMASK(4, 0), 0x17);
+	/* Set 45ohm HS ODT value to 5'b11111 to increase driver strength */
+	phy_update_bits(rphy->phy_base + 0x11c, GENMASK(4, 0), 0x1f);
 
 	/* Set Tx HS eye height tuning to 3'b011(462 mV)*/
 	phy_update_bits(rphy->phy_base + 0x124, GENMASK(4, 2), (0x03 << 2));
